The issue: `z.grow_to(output_size)`. If `z.size() >= output_size`, `grow_to` does nothing. If `z.size() < output_size`, `grow_to` should resize `z` to at least `output_size`.

But wait - what if `z.size() < output_size` and `m_reg.capacity() < output_size`? Then `grow_to` does `m_reg.resize(round_up(output_size, 8))`. `round_up(output_size, 8)` is always >= output_size. So `z.size() >= output_size`. That's fine.

What if `z.size() < output_size` and `m_reg.capacity() >= output_size`? Then `grow_to` does `m_reg.resize(m_reg.capacity())`. Since `m_reg.capacity() >= output_size`, `z.size() >= output_size`. That's fine.

So `grow_to` always ensures `z.size() >= output_size`. I don't see the bug.

