Actually, let me look at the `BigInt` copy constructor and assignment operator:

```cpp
BigInt::BigInt(const BigInt& other)
   {
   m_reg = other.m_reg;
   m_signedness = other.m_signedness;
   }
```

When `BigInt z = x;` is executed, `z.m_reg = x.m_reg`. The `m_reg` is a `secure_vector<word>`. When you copy a vector, the capacity of the copy is typically equal to the size of the source (or at least >= size). So `z.m_reg.capacity() >= z.m_reg.size() = x.m_reg.size()`.

Now, `z.grow_to(output_size)`. If `output_size > z.size()`, then:
- If `output_size <= z.m_reg.capacity()`, resize to `z.m_reg.capacity()`. Since `z.m_reg.capacity() >= output_size`, this is fine.
- Otherwise, resize to `round_up(output_size, 8)`. Since `round_up(output_size, 8) >= output_size`, this is fine.

So `grow_to` always ensures `z.size() >= output_size`. I really can't see the bug in `grow_to` itself.

